import Mathlib

/-!
# OEIS A243929: numerators of the rational array generated by x+1 and -3/x

Source: https://oeis.org/A243929
Credit: @xor2k
Sibling of OEIS A243925 (same conjecture wording); statement modelled on the accepted one for that entry.

OEIS comment: Decree that (row 1) = (1). For n >= 2, row n consists of numbers in increasing
order generated as follows: x+1 for each x in row n-1 together with -3/x for each nonzero x in
row n-1, where duplicates are deleted as they occur. Conjecture: every rational number occurs
exactly once in the array.

A243929 lists the numerators of this array, read by rows (A243928 gives the denominators,
A243930 the row lengths); the conjecture is about the array itself. The comment's remark that
the number of numbers in row n is A243930(n) is a definition, not a conjecture.

Conventions: `seen 3 n` is the set of all rationals in rows 1, ..., n+1 of the array (the source's
row 1 corresponds to index 0). Generating from all earlier rows gives the same union as
generating from the last row only, since the images of earlier rows already lie in earlier
unions. Deleting duplicates as they occur means a rational belongs to the row of its earliest
generation, so `row 3 n` is the set of rationals whose least index in `seen 3` is `n`. The order
inside a row is irrelevant to the conjecture, so rows are sets.
-/

namespace OMA.S_oeis_a243929

/-- All rationals produced at depth at most n. -/
def seen (d : ℚ) : ℕ → Set ℚ
  | 0 => {1}
  | n+1 => seen d n ∪ ((fun x : ℚ => x+1) '' seen d n) ∪
        ((fun x : ℚ => -d/x) '' (seen d n \ {0}))

/-- The rationals whose first appearance is at depth `n` (row `n + 1` of the source). -/
def row (d : ℚ) (n : ℕ) : Set ℚ :=
  {x | IsLeast {k | x ∈ seen d k} n}

/-- Every rational occurs in a unique row of the array. -/
theorem main : ∀ x : ℚ, ∃! n, x ∈ row 3 n := by
  sorry

end OMA.S_oeis_a243929
